{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774293697,"nonce":"rH59YzokuVCXa//7HM8UqHiQZHs=","title":"Studio: training method control for CVaR and adversarial training (APT)","message":"## Summary\n\nFeedbax Studio currently uses baseline backprop for training. RLRMP needs support for alternative loss aggregation and adversarial training methods to study robust motor control.\n\n## Two Methods to Support\n\n### CVaR (easy)\nConditional Value-at-Risk: sample K realizations per condition, sort losses, backprop worst (1-alpha) fraction. This is just a change in loss aggregation — no new trainer, no new graph components. Could be exposed as a parameter on the existing training configuration: e.g., `cvar_alpha: 0.9` (optimize for worst 10%).\n\nStudio integration: add a field to the training config panel. Minimal work.\n\n### APT — Adversarial Perturbation Training (harder)\nInner gradient ascent loop on the perturbation signal, finding the worst-case perturbation subject to a norm constraint, then outer loop updates network parameters. This requires:\n- An inner optimization loop within each training step\n- The inner loop is model-specific (gradient of loss w.r.t. perturbation depends on the forward dynamics graph)\n- The norm constraint depends on the perturbation type and amplitude schedule\n\nStudio integration options:\n1. **Simplest**: enum selection (standard / CVaR / APT) with APT requiring per-model custom code\n2. **Medium**: APT as a higher-order training wrapper that takes the existing training graph and adds the inner loop automatically (if the perturbation injection point is known from the model graph)\n3. **Future**: represent the training procedure itself as a graph (training graph related to model graph) — this is general but complex\n\n## Design Question\n\nThe training procedure could eventually be representable as a graph. For now, an enum/config selection probably suffices. APT's model-specificity is the main challenge — it needs to know where perturbations enter the forward dynamics and how to differentiate through them.\n\n## Relationship to Existing Work\n\n- Related to ba00cef (alternative training methods) but distinct: CVaR and APT are backprop-based, not RL/ES\n- Motivated by rlrmp issues 84ee4ff and c05998e\n- e57ae1f (wire Studio training to real JAX training) is a prerequisite\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.6 \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e","files":null}]}